From 8713e320b021c4067912f5a8bf06229eed152963 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 03:30:23 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] ui, zeo_i18n: Zed's own labels, buttons and tooltips ask the
 catalog (spike)

Zed has no translation system and its strings are English literals in
about 255 crates; a t!() at each of the ~1,700 call sites would conflict on
every bump. Instead the constructors that take display text -- Label::new,
Button::new's label and the Tooltip titles -- pass it through
ui::zeo_text::translate. The ui crate holds only a function pointer, empty
until zeo_i18n::init installs core_text::translate, so tests render the
English they always did.

core_text finds a text under an id derived from the English itself, zed-
plus its FNV-1a 64 hash, in locales/<language>/zed-core.ftl; en-US's is
generated by zed-patches/scripts/i18n-extract.py (5,120 strings at
6ba65ce3). The English entry is compared before an id is trusted, so a hash
collision keeps the English. A memo per language means Fluent formats a
text once, not every frame; in English the cost is one atomic load.
ZEO_I18N_COVERAGE=<file> writes one hit/miss line per distinct text seen,
which is how the spike measures coverage. Text built at run time has no id
and stays English.
---

